Below is a complete, step-by-step recipe for “on-the-fly” icons in **MapLibre GL JS** when the style’s sprite does not contain the requested image.  
The core idea is to listen for the `styleimagemissing` event, fetch or generate the image yourself, and register it with `map.addImage()`. Once the image is added, any layer that references the same `icon-image` name will instantly start drawing the new icon.

---

## 1️⃣ Why the error happens

When a layer’s `layout.icon-image` (or `layout.text-field` with an `icon-` token) refers to a name that is **not** present in the style’s sprite, MapLibre throws a `StyleImageMissingError` and the layer is rendered with a transparent placeholder.  
The library deliberately does **not** silently ignore the missing image because it wants you to know that the style is incomplete.

> **Key point:** The error is emitted as a **`styleimagemissing`** event, which gives you a perfect hook to supply the missing asset yourself.

---

## 2️⃣ The “official” way – `styleimagemissing` event

```js
// Assume `map` is an already-created MapLibre GL JS instance
map.on('load', () => {
  // 1️⃣ Register a global handler for any missing image
  map.on('styleimagemissing', async (e) => {
    const missingId = e.id;               // the name the style tried to use
    console.warn(`Missing icon "${missingId}" – loading it now…`);

    try {
      // 2️⃣ Load the image (you can fetch from a CDN, a data-uri, a canvas, etc.)
      const image = await loadIcon(missingId); // <-- see helper below

      // 3️⃣ Register it with the map
      //    `sdf: true` if you want to tint the icon with `icon-color`
      map.addImage(missingId, image, { sdf: false, pixelRatio: 2 });

      // 4️⃣ (Optional) Force a repaint if the layer was already drawn
      //    Usually not needed – MapLibre will automatically re-render.
    } catch (err) {
      console.error(`Failed to load icon "${missingId}":`, err);
    }
  });

  // …add your layers that reference icons here
});
```

### What the handler receives

| Property | Meaning |
|----------|---------|
| `e.id`   | The **icon name** the style tried to resolve (e.g. `"my-custom-pin"`). |
| `e.source` | The source that requested the image (rarely needed). |
| `e.type` | Always `"styleimagemissing"` – useful if you attach a generic listener. |

---

## 3️⃣ Helper: `loadIcon(id)` – fetch / generate the image

You can implement `loadIcon` in many ways. Below are three common patterns:

#### a) Load from a remote URL (e.g. a CDN)

```js
async function loadIcon(id) {
  // Map your icon IDs to URLs however you like
  const urlMap = {
    'airport':      'https://my.cdn.com/icons/airport.svg',
    'restaurant':   'https://my.cdn.com/icons/restaurant.png',
    // fallback for any unknown id
    'default':      'https://my.cdn.com/icons/default.png',
  };
  const url = urlMap[id] ?? urlMap['default'];

  // MapLibre ships with a tiny wrapper that returns an ImageBitmap
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    map.loadImage(url, (error, image) => {
      if (error) return reject(error);
      resolve(image); // `image` is an ImageBitmap ready for addImage()
    });
  });
}
```

#### b) Load a **Base64-encoded** data-uri (useful for tiny icons)

```js
async function loadIcon(id) {
  const dataUriMap = {
    'star': 'data:image/svg+xml;base64,PHN2ZyB4bWxucz0iaHR0cDovL3d3dy53...'
    // …
  };
  const dataUri = dataUriMap[id] ?? dataUriMap['fallback'];
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    map.loadImage(dataUri, (err, img) => err ? reject(err) : resolve(img));
  });
}
```

#### c) Generate on-the-fly with a `<canvas>` (e.g. coloured circles, text)

```js
async function loadIcon(id) {
  // Example: a coloured circle whose colour is encoded in the id: "circle-#ff0000"
  const match = id.match(/^circle-(#[0-9a-f]{6})$/i);
  if (!match) throw new Error('Unsupported dynamic icon id');

  const colour = match[1];
  const size = 64; // pixel size of the icon

  const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
  canvas.width = canvas.height = size;
  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

  // Draw a simple filled circle
  ctx.fillStyle = colour;
  ctx.beginPath();
  ctx.arc(size / 2, size / 2, size / 2 - 2, 0, Math.PI * 2);
  ctx.fill();

  // Convert canvas → ImageBitmap (required by addImage)
  return await createImageBitmap(canvas);
}
```

> **Tip:** `map.loadImage()` works with **any** URL that the browser can resolve (including `blob:` URLs you create from a `File` or `ArrayBuffer`). If you already have an `HTMLImageElement`, `HTMLCanvasElement`, or `ImageBitmap`, you can pass it directly to `map.addImage()` – no need to go through `loadImage`.

---

## 4️⃣ Adding the image *before* the layer is created (optional)

If you know ahead of time which icons you’ll need, you can pre-load them **once** and then add the layers. This avoids the “flash of missing-icon” that can happen when the layer renders before the async fetch finishes.

```js
async function preloadIcons(iconIds) {
  await Promise.all(iconIds.map(async (id) => {
    const img = await loadIcon(id);
    map.addImage(id, img, { sdf: false });
  }));
}

// Example usage:
map.on('load', async () => {
  await preloadIcons(['airport', 'restaurant']);
  // now add layers that reference those icons – they will be instantly available
});
```

---

## 5️⃣ Handling **SDF** (Signed Distance Field) icons

If you want to tint the icon with `icon-color` (or use `icon-opacity`), you need to add the image as an **SDF** image:

```js
map.addImage('my-sdf-icon', imgBitmap, { sdf: true, pixelRatio: 2 });
```

*Make sure the source SVG/PNG is actually an SDF (i.e. a monochrome image where the shape is encoded as a distance field). Otherwise the tinting will look wrong.*

---

